  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d6/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_55.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man and the Wasp are watching clips of their previous encounter with the Human Top (see [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14]-51).&nbsp; They ponder how difficult it was to capture him.&nbsp; But they cannot know that miles away, the Human Top is using his spinning powers to escape from prison!&nbsp; After re-donning his uniform, he needs money, so he heads for a bank.&nbsp; Hank and Jan see this reported on the news and immediately spring into action.&nbsp; But they arrive too late to prevent the heist.\n\nBut while Giant-Man and the Wasp are heading home, as fate would have it, the Human Top happens to be heading in the same direction and sees them.&nbsp; When they get home (not realizing they've been followed), the have a normal spat, overheard by the Top outside the window, who realizes how heart-warming it is \"to see two people who are deeply in love that way!\"\n\nHe then swoops in and grabs the belt off of Giant-Man, where he keeps his growth capsules!&nbsp; The Top has found them, and he immediately takes one of the growth capsules and becomes a giant whirlwind.&nbsp; Hank is hurled into a closet, which the Top locks him inside, and he uses his speed to capture the Wasp inside a small jar.&nbsp; He warns Giant-Man to lay off, takes the Wasp and heads out.\n\nBut Giant-Man still has his cybernetic helmet, which he uses to summon ants, who ultimately bring him a shrinking capsule so he can escape under the door.&nbsp; In moments, he's back in action as Giant-Man, and since the Human Top is still giant-sized himself, it's easy for him to track him down.&nbsp; The two battle briefly before Giant-Man instructs some nearby termites to weaken the roof they're on, and the Top crashes through (lucky it's a \"deserted tenement\" - heh, \"the old deserted building trick!\")\n\nWhile the Top is unconscious, the Wasp pops a shrinking pill into his mouth, and he's recaptured, swearing that the \"next time'll be <i>different</i>!\"\n\nThe newspapers cover the event, and Hank points out Wasp's name in print, leaving Jan to think \"Although I'd rather see it on a <i>wedding license</i>, you big, blind, wonderful goof!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "a passable story.&nbsp; The Human Top is not terribly inventive, but he's at least more interesting than the other villains Giant-Man and the Wasp have faced.&nbsp; But this is hardly redemption for this title, which I have to confess I mostly dread picking up - it's been a <i>major</i> disappointment thus far, but let's give credit where it's due.&nbsp; This issue's plot was...<i>okay</i>.&nbsp; It won't set the world on fire and have people clamoring to reserve copies, but it's readable.\n\nI continue to enjoy how Hank acts as though they have only a professional partnership even though it's obvious to everyone (even the Top, listening outside a window) that he's actually in love with the Wasp.&nbsp; Of course, the Wasp herself makes no attempt to hide her amorous feelings toward Hank.\n\nThe Giant-Man and Wasp fan club is a bit goofy.\n\nThe last panel of page 5 was unfortunately drawn.&nbsp; When I first looked at it, it appeared that Giant-Man had grown so large as to have to carefully step around cars on the street, and I thought he's grown taller than ever before, particularly when the Wasp says \"Wait! Stop!&nbsp; You---you'll <i>kill</i> yourself, Hank!\" (which I thought referred to him growing too tall), but it turns out he was actually throwing himself out the open window many stories up with the intent to grab hold of the cable he attached to the reinforced flag pole, about which I can only add - boy, he better <i>hope</i> he doesn't miss grabbing that loop on the way down!&nbsp; <i>Geez!</i>\n\nAs for being locked in the closet, this has always struck me as very poor - the idea of putting a lock with a key on a closet door, particularly one that then cannot be opened from the inside!&nbsp; What a horrible design!&nbsp; I get that from the <i>outside</i> you might want to require a key so you can lock the door, preventing access, but what designer in his right mind would not provide an easy way out from the <i>inside</i> in the event that an older sibling (on a bad day) might lock his or her annoying kid brother in the closet!&nbsp; <i>Oh brother!</i>\n\nAnd Jan being trapped in a glass jar?&nbsp; Seriously?&nbsp; Is that all it takes to capture her?&nbsp; Is she really helpless just because she's in a jar?&nbsp; I know she doesn't get strength enhancement until <i>way</i> later (see <i>Marvel Team-Up</i> 59-60), but even a normal sized human might be able to crack a glass jar.&nbsp; I also can't recall if she has a wasp's sting yet, but if she does, that too might be helpful to escape!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"The Gypsy's Secret!\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, L. D. Lieber and George Bell)\n\nWhen the Wasp interrupts another of Hank's experiments, she declares that he wouldn't be satisfied even if he <i>found</i> what he was looking for, and to illustrate, she begins telling him a tale about an old gypsy named Gorko who uses alchemy in order to turn lead into gold.&nbsp; But even though it could transform his life, he chooses to live humbly and simply in his trailer, all the while searching for <i>something</i> -- in fact, the \"<i>only</i> thing of importance to me!\"\n\nBut one day, his wagon gets stuck in the mud on a road, and he's forced to request assistance at \"a nearby castle\" where a man named Baron Radzik lives.&nbsp; At first, he refuses to help the old man, and when he offers the Baron gold, he simply assumes the gypsy must have <i>stolen</i> it!\n\nBut when the gypsy indicates he can turn lead into gold, the Baron demands he reveal the technique -- the old man <i>refuses</i> to tell one whose heart is filled with such greed.&nbsp; Consequently, the Baron tosses the gypsy into his dungeon until such time as he reveals the secret!\n\nRelying on his wits, the gypsy lies and claims that the technique is too complicated to commit to memory and tells the Baron that he has it written down in his wagon home.&nbsp; The two <i>immediately</i> set out for the wagon.\n\nBut when the Baron enters and finds it dark, he is suddenly trapped by iron bars, and the wagon sheds its outer form and reveals that it is a spaceship, which rockets into the air.&nbsp; To his horror, the Baron learns that the gypsy is <i>really</i> an alien, who is from the \"ninth galaxy,\" sent to Earth to collect a human specimen for an \"intergalactic zoo.\"\n\nBaron Radzik is to be that specimen, for who on Earth would ever miss so greedy a person as he, or \"even shed a tear?\"&nbsp; The alien then assures him that he will indeed share his gold formula, but it will do him little good since gold is a very common metal on his world.\n\nAnd with the moral lesson told (that one can get what one wants and gain no benefit from it), the Wasp concludes.&nbsp; But she learns that her story has only given Hank a <i>new</i> idea:&nbsp; that of discovering the \"ancient formula for turning lead into gold!\"\n\nIt appears there will be no dinner and dancing tonight.&nbsp; She turns sadly away."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
